  "body": "**What was wrong**\n\nFor loans with Quarterly repayments, the interest for every payment after the first one was calculated using 3 days instead of the real number of days in that quarter (which is 90, 91, or 92 days).\n\nThis is a common mistake in financial modeling: mixing up \"how many periods\" with \"how many days in a period\". A quarter means \"pay every 3 months\", but 3 months is not a fixed number of days, so you cannot use \"3\" directly in a day-based interest formula. Getting this wrong leads to mispriced loans and a broken amortization schedule, because each later payment builds on the balance left by the one before it.\n\nThe code had a function that returns how many days to use for interest. For Quarterly, it returned a flat \"3\". That \"3\" was meant to mean \"3 months\", not \"3 days\", but it was being used directly as a day count in the interest formula. This made quarterly interest about 30 times too small after the first payment, and every balance after that was wrong too.\n\n**What changed**\n\nChanged the Quarterly case to use the real number of days between the current payment date and the previous payment date in the schedule.\n\nThe first version of this fix calculated the previous date by going back 3 months from the current one. That looked right in most cases, but it broke for loans starting near the end of a month (29th, 30th, 31st), because going back 3 months on its own does not always land on the same date the schedule actually used earlier. So instead, the code now keeps track of the real previous payment date as it builds the schedule, and uses that.\n\n**Testing**\n\nAdded a test that creates a Quarterly loan starting on the 31st (to catch the end-of-month case), submits it, disburses it, and checks that every row uses the correct number of days, and that the loan balance reaches zero at the end.\n\nAlso manually checked the exact numbers reported by a user (1,000,000 principal, 12% interest, 8 quarterly payments starting 2026-04-01) and confirmed the schedule now shows the right interest.<hr>This is an automatic backport of pull request #1483 done by [Mergify](https://mergify.com).\n",
